<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lacquer">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lacquerware</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 7000 BCE</span> · Also known as Lacquer, Urushi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">Lacquerware</a> consists of objects coated with lacquer, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> an urushiol-based lacquer from the dried sap of the lacquer tree, Toxicodendron vernicifluum. The oldest known lacquerware, burial ornaments from an Initial Jōmon pit grave at the Kakinoshima site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, is about 9,000 years old, and the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is a red wooden bowl from a Hemudu culture site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>. Chinese lacquer became a decorative art under the Shang dynasty and a commercial industry in the state of Chu under the Eastern Zhou, and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and Japan; the traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> arose independently.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lacquer.jpg" alt="Lacquerware" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liubomir G · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Музей_азійського_мистецтва9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 7000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Kakinoshima, Hakodate, Hokkaido, Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Zhejiang, China (Hemudu culture) (5th millennium BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Kakinoshima pieces are also dated to the 9th millennium BCE, and a lacquer tree of the Jōmon period is 12,600 years old. Experts are divided on whether Jōmon lacquer was invented independently or derived from Chinese techniques.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=lacquer">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
